<TopicPage slug="subjects/design/intermediate/accessibility">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Accessibility is <strong>the design lesson's ethics made technical</strong>: a spec (WCAG),
    a law (equality acts) and a craft (inclusive patterns) — with the recurring surprise
    that building for the edges builds better for everyone. The foundational lessons
    established that design begins with user needs; this page takes that principle to its
    honest conclusion, because the hardest user needs to serve are the ones the design
    team does not personally have. The World Health Organization counts about
    <strong>1.3 billion people — roughly 1 in 6 of us — living with significant disability</strong>,
    and that is before counting temporary and situational limits: the arm in a cast, the
    new parent carrying a baby, the commuter in bright sunlight. This page covers the
    spectrum of ability designers actually design for, the two mechanical pillars of web
    accessibility (contrast and semantics), the ergonomics that decide whether a physical
    product is usable at all, and the curb-cut economics that make inclusion the best
    deal in design.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="POUR, the mechanics, design as access, curb cuts" widget="equationStep" config={a11yCfg} />

  <h2>The spectrum of ability: permanent, temporary, situational</h2>
  <p>
    The field's most useful mental model rejects the disabled/everyone binary. Every
    capability exists on a spectrum — vision, hearing, dexterity, cognition — and a
    person's position on it changes across hours and years. A <strong>permanent</strong>
    limitation is one-handed typing after a stroke; a <strong>temporary</strong> one is a
    broken wrist; a <strong>situational</strong> one is holding a hot drink. Microsoft's
    inclusive-design toolkit popularised this framing, and its practical force is that
    <em>everyone</em> is a customer of accessibility: captions serve deaf users, then
    gym-goers watching muted screens; voice control serves users with motor
    impairments, then drivers and cooks. Designing for the "default user" is therefore
    a statistical fiction — there is no body, device or context that represents the
    middle of every spectrum at once. The professional question is not "does the design
    work for people like me?" but "where does it start to fail, and can we push that
    boundary out?".
  </p>

  <h2>Contrast: the arithmetic of legibility</h2>
  <p>
    The first mechanical pillar is contrast between text and its background, and the
    Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) make it exact: <strong>4.5:1 for normal
    body text</strong>, <strong>3:1 for large text</strong> (roughly 24 px, or 19 px bold) and
    for UI components and meaningful graphics. The ratio is computed from
    <em>relative luminance</em> — a weighted blend of the red, green and blue channels
    that approximates how human vision perceives brightness (green contributes most,
    blue least) — as (lighter + 0.05) ÷ (darker + 0.05). The scale runs from 1:1
    (identical, invisible) to 21:1 (black on white). A worked example fixes intuition:
    the grey <strong>#767676</strong> on white works out to about
    (1.0 + 0.05) ÷ (0.18 + 0.05) ≈ <strong>4.55:1</strong> — just over the line, which is
    why #767676 is famously "the lightest grey that passes" for body text, and any
    lighter placeholder-grey fails. Two practical consequences follow. First,
    nobody can judge contrast by eye — a 4.4:1 pairing and a 4.6:1 pairing are
    indistinguishable to intuition but legally and functionally different — so contrast
    checkers are part of every professional palette. Second, WCAG 2.2's most-cited
    revision (3:1 for focus indicators) is really a contrast rule about <em>where am I
    on this page?</em> — the keyboard user's position marker must be visible against
    both its background states.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> accessibility means obeying contrast rules at the
  cost of brand aesthetics. The constraint does the opposite of degrading design:
  it forces the hierarchy the typography lesson preached — large type, weight
  variation, spacing and a limited, high-contrast palette are exactly what strong
  visual design already looks like. Light-grey-on-white body text is not a style;
  it is a legibility debt someone else has to pay.</p>

  <h2>Semantics: the page that machines (and blind users) can read</h2>
  <p>
    The second pillar is invisible on screen: the <strong>structure of the document</strong>.
    Screen readers — the software blind users navigate with — do not read pages top to
    bottom like a fax machine; they move through structure: jump heading to heading,
    leap to the main landmark, list the links, announce "button, Search" as a single
    object. That navigation is only possible when the page is built <strong>semantically</strong>:
    real headings in order, real lists, real buttons and links, labels bound to the
    form fields they name. A page that looks identical but is assembled from generic
    boxes with hand-applied styles is, to a screen reader, an undifferentiated stream
    of text — a city without street signs. The same semantics power the page's other
    machine audiences: search engines, reader modes and browser extensions all
    restructure content through the same tags. Alongside structure sits
    <strong>alt text</strong>, whose discipline is to convey <em>function, not appearance</em>:
    "Search" for a magnifying-glass icon, not "magnifying glass"; a concise description
    for an informative chart; an empty alt attribute (the image is decoration) rather
    than a duplicated caption the user hears twice. And underneath everything,
    <strong>keyboard operability</strong>: every action reachable with Tab, Enter, arrows
    and a visible focus indicator, because motor-impaired users, screen-reader users
    and power users share one truth — if it cannot be reached, it does not exist.
  </p>

  <h2>Anthropometrics and ergonomics: designing for every body</h2>
  <p>
    Physical products carry the same duties through measurement rather than markup.
    <strong>Anthropometrics</strong> is the study of body measurements, and its first lesson is
    that populations vary enormously: designs therefore aim at the <strong>5th to 95th
    percentile</strong> of the relevant measures rather than the average, since a design
    fitted to the mean fails roughly half the population in one direction or the
    other. The standard worked thinking: a doorway must clear the tallest users;
    a shelf, the reach envelope of the shortest; a handle's grip diameter must suit
    both large and small hands, which is why tool makers moved toward fatter, softer,
    non-cylindrical grips and why lever taps displaced crosshead taps in public
    buildings — a twisting grip excludes arthritic hands that a downward lever
    accommodates. <strong>Ergonomics</strong> adds the fit between body and product in use:
    controls within the comfortable reach zone (roughly the sweep of the forearm
    without shoulder extension), display information readable without stooping,
    forces that do not demand peak grip strength. The accessibility translation is
    direct — <strong>step-free access, adequate turning space for wheelchairs (about
    1.5 m diameter), controls operable one-handed and without tight pinching or
    twisting</strong> — and none of it helps only wheelchair users or people with
    arthritis: it helps the delivery worker with a trolley and the customer
    carrying a suitcase.
  </p>

  <h2>Safety, inclusivity and the curb-cut effect</h2>
  <p>
    The syllabus groups safety and inclusivity with aesthetics, and the grouping is
    the point: inclusive design is not a separate discipline bolted on after the
    beautiful version is finished — it <em>is</em> the aesthetic, in the way the
    accessibility-grade type hierarchy and the lever tap are. History's clearest
    evidence is the <strong>curb cut</strong>. Disability activists in Berkeley in the
    1970s poured informal ramps at street corners; the idea went mainstream, and
    kerb ramps are now legally required — after which prams, wheelchairs, suitcases,
    delivery trolleys and bikes followed through them. The pattern repeated: TV
    captions migrated from a deaf-audience accommodation to a default for noisy
    environments; voice assistants built for accessibility found kitchens; the
    first typewriter with a readable typebar arrangement came out of work for
    blind users (Pellegrino Turri's 1808 machine, built for a blind countess).
    Each case teaches the same economics: <strong>features designed for the edges
    become infrastructure for the middle</strong>, and the market impact is not
    charity — one widely cited analysis (Return on Disability) estimates the
    disposable income of people with disabilities and their households at over
    $13 trillion globally.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> accessibility is a small-market concern, a niche
  within the user base. The spectrum model dissolves the niche: add the permanent
  16% to the temporarily injured and situationally limited, then count the aging
  populations every market is drifting toward, and "the edge" turns out to be a
  rotating position everyone occupies part of the time.</p>

  <h2>Testing: automation is the smoke alarm, humans the fire brigade</h2>
  <p>
    A design is not accessible because the team intended it; it is accessible because
    it survives testing. The standard pipeline has tiers. <strong>Automated checkers</strong>
    (axe, Lighthouse, the browser's own audits) catch the mechanical failures — missing
    alt attributes, contrast violations, missing labels — in seconds, but research and
    vendor documentation agree such tools flag only around <strong>30–40% of WCAG
    issues</strong>; the structural failures (nonsensical heading order, focus traps,
    unlabelled custom widgets) need judgement. <strong>Keyboard-only passes</strong> catch
    operability failures: unplug the mouse and try to complete the task. <strong>Screen-reader
    sessions</strong> (NVDA or JAWS on Windows, VoiceOver on Apple platforms) catch the
    semantic ones — and they take practice, since the first hour of screen-reader use
    is disorienting for exactly the reasons the design should not be. Finally,
    <strong>testing with disabled users</strong>, recruited through the same research
    methods as the UX research lesson, replaces assumptions with evidence; the
    professional norm is to pay participants for their expertise, because that is
    what it is.
  </p>

  <h2>Cognitive access and plain language</h2>
  <p>
    The least visible accessibility frontier is the sentence itself. Roughly one in
    five adults reads below the level most websites are written at — including
    many with dyslexia, ADHD, second-language readers and tired commuters — and
    cognitive load is an access barrier exactly as real as a missing ramp. The
    plain-language discipline is mechanical, not condescending: <strong>one idea per
    sentence</strong>, common words over jargon ("use" before "utilise"), the
    active voice, and the point of a paragraph stated first (the inverted-pyramid
    discipline, applied to documentation). Structure does half the work: real
    headings every few paragraphs, lists for genuine lists, and — the web
    accessibility study that refuses to die — links that make sense out of
    context ("the annual report" rather than "click here"), because screen-reader
    users navigate by pulling a list of link labels. Forms carry the highest
    cognitive load in any product, so the rules concentrate there: label every
    field visibly (placeholders disappear — they are not labels), make errors
    human ("Enter your postcode, like CB4 1HT") and forgiving (preserve
    everything the user typed), and let deadlines and timeouts be extended.
    None of this costs the fluent reader anything — which is the recurring
    lesson of the whole field: the inclusive version is almost always the
    better version for everyone.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The overlay cautionary tale.</strong> A whole industry sells bolt-on
    "accessibility" widgets: a script that injects alt text, font resizers and
    contrast toggles onto an inaccessible site. The accessibility profession's
    consensus — expressed in a 2021 open letter signed by hundreds of practitioners
    and in subsequent litigation — is that overlays do not deliver the conformance
    they advertise and can actively break screen-reader use; real accessibility is
    designed in. The lesson generalises: access is a property of the design, not
    a patch on it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Conformance, levels and law.</strong> WCAG grades success criteria at A,
    AA and AAA; most legislation lands on AA. The legal floor is rising: court
    settlements in the US under the ADA have run into the thousands per year since
    the 2010s, and the European Accessibility Act, applying from <strong>June 2025</strong>,
    brings e-commerce, banking, transport and publishing within accessibility
    requirements across EU member states. Cognitive accessibility is the field's
    live frontier — plain language, predictable navigation, forgiving forms and
    reduced-motion support address the largest disability group but have the fewest
    automatable checks. And the open question is AI: automatic alt-text and
    captioning are genuinely useful drafts, yet their failure modes are silent ones,
    so the current professional consensus treats machine-generated access content
    as a starting point that still needs human review.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> WCAG 2.2 (W3C); WebAIM surveys; Microsoft Inclusive Design toolkit; WHO World Report on Disability; the curb-cut literature; the law lesson's compliance logic.</p>

